"""Mounted under /api/ by config/urls.py, so the full paths are

    /api/webhooks/conecto/ticket-created/
    /api/webhooks/conecto/ticket-replied/
    /api/webhooks/conecto/ticket-closed/
    /api/webhooks/conecto/client-registered/
    /api/webhooks/conecto/job-status-changed/
    /api/webhooks/conecto/job-checklist-completed/
    /api/webhooks/conecto/technician-position-updated/
    /api/webhooks/conecto/internal-ticket-created/
    /api/webhooks/conecto/internal-ticket-replied/
    /api/webhooks/conecto/ticket-note-added/

Trailing slashes included, because Django's APPEND_SLASH redirect turns
a POST into a GET and would silently lose the body. Conecto must call
them exactly as written above.

One read-only endpoint sits alongside those, for the Scheduling screens:

    /api/technician-positions/

And three that CONECTO calls on us -- the only direction that runs this way
-- for a client watching their live speed in the app:

    POST /api/conecto/bandwidth/start/
    POST /api/conecto/bandwidth/poll/
    POST /api/conecto/bandwidth/stop/
    POST /api/conecto/documents/invoice-url/
    POST /api/conecto/documents/statement-url/

One of ours is deliberately open to the world, because the client's own
phone opens it with no session:

    GET  /api/public/conecto-document/<token>/

Trailing slashes matter on these exactly as much as on the webhooks above,
and for the same reason: APPEND_SLASH answers a slashless POST with a
redirect that the caller follows as a GET, losing the body in silence.
"""
